w_{\Lambda} = \frac{p_{\Lambda}}{\rho_{\Lambda}}.
\]



A cosmological constant satisfies


\[
w_{\Lambda} = -1.
\]



\subsection{59.3 Accelerated Expansion}

Acceleration occurs when


\[
\ddot{a}(t) > 0,
\]


which corresponds to


\[
w < -\frac{1}{3}.
\]



\subsection{59.4 Scalar Field Models}

Quintessence models use a scalar field $\phi$ with potential $V(\phi)$:


\[
\rho_{\phi} = \frac{1}{2}\dot{\phi}^2 + V(\phi).
\]



\subsection{59.5 Gate Interpretation}

Dark energy evolution is governed by a Gate


\[
G_{\Lambda} : \mathcal{G}_{\text{cosmo}} \to \mathcal{G}_{\text{cosmo}}
\]


that modifies the metric Gate:


\[
g_{\mu\nu}(t+1) = G_{\text{metric}}(g_{\mu\nu}(t), \rho_{\Lambda}(t)).
\]



\subsection{59.6 Observational Signatures}

Dark energy affects:
\begin{itemize}
    \item supernova luminosity distances,
    \item baryon acoustic oscillations,
    \item CMB late-time anisotropies,
    \item growth rate of structure.
\end{itemize}
\section{60. Cosmic Acceleration: Observational Constraints}

Observations of the late-time universe constrain the properties of dark
energy and the expansion history.

\subsection{60.1 Supernova Distance Modulus}

Type Ia supernovae measure the luminosity distance


\[
d_L(z) = (1+z) \, D_M(z),
\]


providing constraints on $H(z)$.

\subsection{60.2 Baryon Acoustic Oscillations}

BAO measurements determine the angular diameter distance


\[
D_A(z) = \frac{D_M(z)}{1+z}
\]


and the Hubble parameter at specific redshifts.

\subsection{60.3 CMB Constraints}

The CMB provides:
\begin{itemize}
    \item the sound horizon $r_s$,
    \item the distance to last scattering,
    \item late-time integrated Sachs–Wolfe signatures.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{60.4 Growth Rate of Structure}

The growth rate


\[
f(z) = \frac{d\ln D}{d\ln a}
\]


constrains the interplay between dark energy and gravity.

\subsection{60.5 Gate Interpretation}

Observational constraints restrict the allowed Gate family


\[
G_{\text{metric}},\; G_{\Lambda},\; G_{\delta}
\]


to those consistent with measured expansion and structure growth.

\subsection{60.6 Consistency Conditions}

A viable cosmology must satisfy:


\[
\{C_\ell,\; D_A(z),\; H(z),\; f(z)\} \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{obs}},
\]


the space of observationally allowed configurations.


